WEBVTT

cue-1
00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:09.260
In this hands-on, we'll use Claude Code to
find potential issues, verify them with

cue-2
00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:14.360
tests, and document the evidence before
release. Let's begin by defining the scope

cue-3
00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:15.780
of our final code audit.

cue-4
00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:21.780
Instead of asking Claude Code to inspect
everything, we'll focus on how our React

cue-5
00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:24.480
front end communicates with the Express
API.

cue-6
00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:28.240
This includes authentication, session
handling,

cue-7
00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:30.800
task operations, due dates,

cue-8
00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:32.040
pagination,

cue-9
00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:34.860
error responses, and response limits.

cue-10
00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:40.100
A limited scope helps produce findings
that are specific and testable.

cue-11
00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:45.360
Before starting the audit, we also need to
protect sensitive information.

cue-12
00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:50.560
Our working project may contain
environment variables, logs, or

cue-13
00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:55.160
authentication tokens that should never be
included in an AI investigation. Our

cue-14
00:00:55.160 --> 00:01:01.060
audit workspace is ready, so we'll ask
Claude Code to examine the interaction

cue-15
00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:03.220
between the front end and back end.

cue-16
00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:08.040
We're looking for situations where the two
layers make different assumptions.

cue-17
00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:09.560
For example,

cue-18
00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:13.820
the front end may interpret a date
differently from the back end,

cue-19
00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:18.760
or an API response may expose more
information than expected.

cue-20
00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:25.360
Claude Code identifies several potential
risks and assigns estimated severity

cue-21
00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:25.740
levels.

cue-22
00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:28.180
These include date formatting,

cue-23
00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:30.120
authentication validation,

cue-24
00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:35.340
token storage, registration responses, and
unrestricted queries.

cue-25
00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:41.200
Each finding also includes a suggested
verification method. However,

cue-26
00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:45.540
an AI-generated finding is not proof of a
vulnerability.

cue-27
00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:48.320
Some findings may be genuine,

cue-28
00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:52.260
others may already be prevented by
existing validation,

cue-29
00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:55.600
and some may require a design decision.

cue-30
00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:02.460
We'll investigate the due date issue first
because it has a clear, testable impact

cue-31
00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:07.760
on the user interface. We'll now verify
the due date finding by tracing how a date

cue-32
00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:09.440
moves through the application.

cue-33
00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:12.560
The task form sends a calendar date,

cue-34
00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:15.360
such as January 15th, 2030.

cue-35
00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:21.740
The back end stores that value as a
JavaScript date, representing midnight in

cue-36
00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:22.280
UTC.

cue-37
00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:28.840
When the React task list formats the value
using the browser's local time zone, the

cue-38
00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:30.340
displayed date can change.

cue-39
00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:35.480
For someone in New York, midnight UTC
falls on the previous evening,

cue-40
00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:39.520
so January 15th appears as January 14th.

cue-41
00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:46.700
This is a genuine user-facing defect, but
it may not appear on a computer configured

cue-42
00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:48.360
for India or UTC.

cue-43
00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:54.380
To reproduce it reliably, we'll create a
test that checks the same task in New

cue-44
00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:57.260
York, UTC, and India time zones.

cue-45
00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:00.780
The test fails for New York, confirming
the issue.

cue-46
00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:06.980
We'll then update the task list to format
stored calendar dates using UTC rather

cue-47
00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:08.740
than the browser's local time zone.

cue-48
00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:11.200
Let's run the tests again.

cue-49
00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:16.720
All three time zone cases now pass,
showing that the selected date remains

cue-50
00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:19.600
consistent regardless of the user's
location.

cue-51
00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:26.620
We've moved from an AI-generated suspicion
to a verified defect, a targeted fix, and

cue-52
00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:30.100
a passing regression test. We verified
the due date issue,

cue-53
00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:35.880
but a final audit must account for every
finding, not only the one we fixed.

cue-54
00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:41.640
We'll run the existing back-end tests that
cover login validation, readiness

cue-55
00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:43.820
responses, and duplicate registration.

cue-56
00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:49.700
These checks help us determine which
concerns are genuine and which are already

cue-57
00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:50.240
addressed.

cue-58
00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:56.680
For example, the reference audit initially
flags a possible NoSQL injection risk,

cue-59
00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:01.500
but the validation tests show that
operator-based input is rejected.

cue-60
00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:05.160
Other findings require a different
response.

cue-61
00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:12.120
Storing JWTs in local storage presents a
security trade-off that needs an

cue-62
00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:16.140
architectural decision rather than an
automatic code change.

cue-63
00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:22.019
We'll record every finding in our final
audit document, along with its status,

cue-64
00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:24.660
evidence, and required action.

cue-65
00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:30.580
The important principle is that an
AI-generated severity label does not

cue-66
00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:33.080
establish whether a risk exists.

cue-67
00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:39.240
Our final audit is trustworthy because
each conclusion is supported by source

cue-68
00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:39.860
inspection,

cue-69
00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:44.640
tests, or an explicitly documented
unresolved decision.

cue-70
00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:51.020
We've used Claude Code to identify
potential risks across our React front end

cue-71
00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:52.100
and Express back end,

cue-72
00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:57.820
then manually verified its findings
through source inspection and automated

cue-73
00:04:57.820 --> 00:04:58.100
tests.

cue-74
00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:01.560
Thank you for watching, and I will see you
in the next video.
